POINT 2 -- the two new tests really reach the intended error path.  MET, by
direct instrumentation rather than inference.
  Scratch copy .../scratchpad/f1probe: added fmt.Fprintf probes to
  decryptFileWithIdentity (after the os.ReadFile, at the isAgeEncrypted guard,
  and immediately after decryptData) and to rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity's
  decryptData. Then:
  $ go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestDecryptFailureDoesNotLeavePlaintextCached|
      TestRollbackEncryptionWithDecryptFailureEvictsCachedPlaintext'
      ./internal/services/storage/
    PROBE decryptFileWithIdentity path=.../data.csv len=216 isAge=true
    PROBE decryptFileWithIdentity decryptData err=identity did not match any of
          the recipients: incorrect identity for recipient block
    --- PASS: TestDecryptFailureDoesNotLeavePlaintextCached (1.41s)
    PROBE rollback path=.../f1.csv isAge=true decryptData err=identity did not
          match any of the recipients: incorrect identity for recipient block
    --- PASS: TestRollbackEncryptionWithDecryptFailureEvictsCachedPlaintext
  So in both tests: os.ReadFile succeeded (len=216), isAgeEncrypted was TRUE so
  the helper did NOT return early, decryptData WAS called, and it failed with
  the age identity-mismatch error -- the intended failure, not a read error and
  not an early return. Both tests also require err != nil from the helper
  (migration_cache_test.go:354-356 and, for rollback, the loop's `continue` is
  observed via the on-disk assertion at 452), so a silently-succeeding transform
  would fail the test rather than pass it.

POINT 3 -- assertion is on plaintext CONTENT, not entry absence.  MET.
  Read (migration_cache_test.go):
    line 361: if stillCached && bytes.Contains(entry.data, []byte("55555.55"))
    line 443: if stillCached && bytes.Contains(entry.data, []byte("99999.99"))
  (and the attempt-2 encrypt test, line 283, the same shape with "12345.67").
  Demonstrated, not merely read -- these are the assertions that actually fire
  under mutation:
    mut decrypt/move  -> migration_cache_test.go:362: plaintext still resident
                         in cache after a failed decryption
    mut rollback/move -> migration_cache_test.go:444: plaintext still resident
                         in cache after a failed rollback decryption
    mut encrypt/move  -> migration_cache_test.go:284: plaintext still resident
                         in cache after a failed encryption
  An entry holding other bytes would not trip any of these.

  Specifically checked, per the brief's warning about asserting on an entry
  that was never populated: the cache genuinely holds the PLAINTEXT before the
  failing call.
  (i) The tests assert the precondition rather than assuming it, and the
      assertion is real -- scratch copy .../scratchpad/nopub with publishCache
      turned into a no-op:
      $ go test -v -run '<the three placement tests>' ./internal/services/storage/
        migration_cache_test.go:273: precondition failed: plaintext not cached
                                      before migration           FAIL (encrypt)
        migration_cache_test.go:351: precondition failed: plaintext not cached
                                      before decrypt attempt     FAIL (decrypt)
        migration_cache_test.go:432: setup invariant broken: file should be
                                      cached before rollback     FAIL (rollback)
      So none of the three can reach its post-condition on an unpopulated cache.
  (ii) The tests' preconditions check EXISTENCE only, not content, so I proved
      the content half myself. Scratch copy .../scratchpad/precheck, a test
      replicating each setup exactly and dumping the cached bytes:
        PRECHECK decrypt:  cached bytes="account,balance\nchecking,55555.55\n"
                           containsSecret=true isCiphertext=false
        PRECHECK rollback: cached bytes="account,balance\nsavings,99999.99\n"
                           containsSecret=true isCiphertext=false
      The entry present before the failing call is the decrypted plaintext, not
      the ciphertext. Recorded qualification, not a miss: point 3 concerns the
      post-condition, which is content-based; the precondition's existence-only
      form cannot produce a false PASS because (i) makes an empty cache fatal
      and the mutations in point 4 show the entry holds the secret.

